board as the main strategy","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves password","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about password?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Strong practice: unique passwords, not shared, long and hard to guess;) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Using the same password “Soccer1” on every site means one leak opens many doors","Writing passwords on a sticky note under the keyboard as the main strategy","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about password?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for password?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name unique only, with no linked example","Create a four-rule password poster for your class (unique, length, no sharing, official resets)","Retype the worked example with no new example","Writing passwords on a sticky note under the keyboard as the main strategy"],"question":"Which independent response best applies password to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about password with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating password on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using password on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for password. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with password, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Writing passwords on a sticky note under the keyboard as the main strategy.. Correction: Refuse and use official help.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-information-software-technology-y9-w06-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 IST alignment. Official outcome codes must match the published IST syllabus page; catalogue codes preferred when present. Verification pending. Not a hacking guide. Follow school acceptable-use rules.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise password.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Information and Software Technology","success_criteria":["I can use password with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify unique and explain why it matters.","I can practise manager independently on a new prompt.","I can notice sharing needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong practice: unique passwords, not shared, long and hard to guess; use school-approved managers if provided.","options":["Strong practice: unique passwords, not shared, long and hard to guess","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","Strong practice: unique passwords, not shared, long and hard to guess; use school-approved managers if provided","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about password?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing the same password “Soccer1” on every site means one leak opens many doors. A unique passphrase per important account limits the blast radius.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSafer response if a friend asks for your login: refuse and suggest official reset help, or hand over the password once?\n\nFEEDBACK\nRefuse and use official help. Sharing logins breaks rules and safety.","explain":"Strong practice: unique passwords, not shared, long and hard to guess; use school-approved managers if provided. Never embed passwords in shared screenshots or slideshows. Teachers reset portals through official channels — not through classmates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing passwords on a sticky note under the keyboard as the main strategy. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"One leaked reused password opens many doors.","caption":"Unique passwords limit damage.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Unique</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">per account</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Reuse</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">risk spreads</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Using the same password “Soccer1” on every site means one leak opens many doors. A unique passphrase per important accou Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Using the same password “Soccer1” on every site means one leak opens many doors. A unique passphrase per important accou Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about password?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nUsing the same password “Soccer1” on every site means one leak opens many doors. A unique passphrase per important account limits the blast radius. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Do not share passwords with classmates.